# Ignore rules for the PROJECT ROOT build context, shared by both images.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this file is duplicated -- read before editing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker only auto-loads `.dockerignore` from the ROOT OF THE BUILD CONTEXT.
# Both images here build from the project root, so the file Docker would look
# for is `D:/DATN/.dockerignore` -- not this one. This copy on its own would be
# silently inert.
#
# BuildKit resolves ignore rules in this order:
#   1. `<path-to-dockerfile>.dockerignore`   <- used here
#   2. `<context-root>/.dockerignore`        <- fallback
#
# So the rules that actually apply live in the sidecar files next to each
# Dockerfile:
#   deployment/docker/Dockerfile.backend.dockerignore
#   deployment/docker/Dockerfile.frontend.dockerignore
#
# This file is the canonical reference the sidecars are kept in sync with, and
# is what takes effect if the project later moves to a root-level
# `.dockerignore`. EDIT ALL THREE TOGETHER, or move to a single root-level file
# and delete these.
#
# Requires BuildKit (default since Docker 23). Under the legacy builder the
# sidecars are ignored and the whole context -- datasets included -- is sent.

# --- Version control --------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
**/.git

# --- Datasets and model weights ---------------------------------------------
# The single most important exclusion. `datasets/` holds the raw and augmented
# training images and is easily tens of gigabytes; sending it to the daemon
# would dominate build time even though nothing in either image uses it.
# Weights are excluded for a different reason: they are mounted read-only at
# runtime, so retraining a model does not require rebuilding the image.
datasets/
models/
*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.engine
*.pdparams
*.pdmodel

# --- Runtime data -----------------------------------------------------------
# Uploaded media and the SQLite file live in volumes. Baking a developer's
# local database into a shipped image would be both wrong and a privacy leak.
data/
storage/
uploads/
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# --- Python -----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.venv/
# Anchored patterns only match the project root. The venvs in this repo are
# .venv-ai, .venv-ocr and backend/.venv, none of which the three lines above
# catch -- backend/.venv alone is 2 GB of WINDOWS wheels that would be sent as
# build context and then baked into a LINUX image by `COPY backend/`.
.venv*/
**/.venv/
**/.venv*/
**/venv/
venv/
env/
.Python
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
.tox/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb

# --- Node / frontend --------------------------------------------------------
# node_modules is reinstalled inside the build stage by `npm ci` against the
# lockfile. Copying the host copy in would be slower and would smuggle in
# platform-specific native binaries built for Windows.
frontend/node_modules/
**/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/.vite/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# --- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment values are injected at RUN time by docker-compose. Anything
# copied into an image is readable by anyone who can pull that image, and
# persists in the layer history even if a later layer deletes it.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets/

# --- Documentation and thesis material --------------------------------------
# Needed for the deliverable, never needed by a running container.
# `*.md` matches root-level files only: a `*` never crosses a `/`, so nested
# READMEs such as `ai/inference/README.md` are still copied. They are a few
# kilobytes and keeping them means the module documentation travels with the
# code it documents.
docs/
demo/
*.md
LICENSE

# --- Training and evaluation ------------------------------------------------
# Training runs on Colab/Kaggle GPU (decision AD-06), never inside these
# images. Only `ai/inference` is used at serving time -- but note that
# `ai/training` and `ai/evaluation` are NOT excluded here, because the backend
# image copies the whole `ai/` package and excluding subpackages would break
# `import ai`. They are small (scripts only); the heavy outputs below are what
# actually matter.
runs/
wandb/
mlruns/
lightning_logs/
*.log

# --- Editor and OS ----------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Docker itself ----------------------------------------------------------
# `deployment/` is deliberately NOT excluded: the frontend image copies
# `deployment/docker/nginx.conf` out of the context, and excluding the
# directory then re-including one file through a `!` exception is a subtle
# rule that breaks the build outright if it is ever evaluated differently.
# The whole directory is a handful of kilobytes, so there is nothing to gain.
docker-compose*.yml

# --- CI ---------------------------------------------------------------------
.github/
.gitlab-ci.yml
